<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">John I Doukas</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">c. 1240–<a href="/years/1289">1289</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/13th-century">13th century</a> · Also known as John I Doukas of Thessaly · Of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/angelos-dynasty">Angelos dynasty</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">John I Doukas was ruler of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/thessaly">Thessaly</a> from about 1268 until his death, an illegitimate son of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/michael-ii-komnenos-doukas">Michael II of Epirus</a>. Ruling from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ypati">Neopatras</a> with the Byzantine title of sebastokrator, he resisted Emperor <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/michael-viii-palaiologos">Michael VIII Palaiologos</a>, defeating a Byzantine army near Neopatras around 1275, and allied with the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/latin">Latin</a> states of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/greece">Greece</a>. His sons Constantine and John II succeeded him.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/john-i-doukas.jpg" alt="John I Doukas" width="320" height="501" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Кардам · CC BY-SA 4.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Ioannes_Doukas.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Early life and background</h2><p>John was apparently the eldest son of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/michael-ii-komnenos-doukas">Michael II Komnenos Doukas</a>, Despot of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/epirus">Epirus</a>, possibly by a mistress of the Gangrenos family. His full surname was Doukas Komnenos Angelos, and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/latin">Latin</a> writers, mistaking the name Doukas for a title, called him Duke of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ypati">Neopatras</a>. He first appears in 1259, married to a daughter of the Thessalian Vlach chieftain Taronas, leading Vlach troops before the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/battle-of-pelagonia">Battle of Pelagonia</a>. According to George Pachymeres and Marino Sanudo Torcello, Achaean knights coveted his wife and William II Villehardouin insulted him for his illegitimate birth, and he deserted to the Nicaeans, contributing to the allies' defeat.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Reign</h2><p>He soon left the Nicaeans, was reconciled with his father and helped him recover Arta and relieve <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ioannina">Ioannina</a>. On Michael II's death his realm was divided: Nikephoros took <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/epirus">Epirus</a> proper and John the lands in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/thessaly">Thessaly</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/central-greece">Central Greece</a>, from Mount Olympus to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mount-parnassus">Mount Parnassus</a>. Michael VIII Palaiologos married a daughter of John to his nephew Andronikos Tarchaneiotes, but John allied with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/charles-i-of-anjou">Charles of Anjou</a>, with whom he made a commercial treaty in April 1273, and with the Duchy of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/athens">Athens</a>.</p><p>He opposed the Union of the Churches concluded at the Council of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lyon">Lyons</a> in 1274. In 1275 a Byzantine army surprised him at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ypati">Neopatras</a>; he slipped through the lines disguised as a groom looking for a stray horse, obtained 300 horsemen from John I de la <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hoffmann-la-roche">Roche</a>, Duke of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/athens">Athens</a>, and scattered the besiegers. On 1 May 1277 a synod at Neopatras anathematised the emperor, the patriarch and the pope, and a synod at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hagia-sophia">Hagia Sophia</a> answered in kind. Another imperial army was later ambushed and crushed at Pharsalos.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Death and legacy</h2><p>After Andronikos II abandoned the Union, Nikephoros and his wife Anna lured John's son Michael to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/epirus">Epirus</a> and had him sent to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/constantinople">Constantinople</a>, where he died in prison; John retaliated by raiding around Arta. He died in or shortly before March 1289, not in 1296 as historians long believed after Karl Hopf, and was buried in the monastery of Porta Panagia, which he had founded in 1283. His widow recognised the nominal suzerainty of Andronikos II to protect their young sons.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “John I Doukas of Thessaly” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>On the death of Michael II of the Despotate of Epirus around 1268, his illegitimate son <b>John I Doukas</b> received Thessaly, with its capital at Neopatras.</blockquote><a href="/state/principality-of-thessaly">Principality of Thessaly</a></li></ul></section>
